76106 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f441e707-bca4-5383-a476-aeddd2c81a22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{1}{x-2}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "498a8787-d5a7-5977-8e3c-6512904632e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176415 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $x=-4$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4) & =(x-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+4 & =2x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x-4 \\\\ x+4-x & =2x-4-x \\\\ 4 & =x-4 \\\\ 4+4 & =x-4+4 \\\\ 8 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=8$ is different from $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $x=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1061 143532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecfccb89-86a2-557a-b9d3-c0ad225826b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1061 143533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36c668d7-ed4b-5afb-8e0b-ee04536f38df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1061 143537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d7e1964-4293-5a03-90c4-211bf2cd63f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1061 143541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe911541-f3cd-5e52-abb4-31aa92a738cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{x+3} = \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that when the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nHowever, before we start, note that $x=-3$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions. This is because when $x=-3,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side, and when $x=2,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the right-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{x+3} & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ 12\\cdot (x-2) & =(x+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12x-24 & =2x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-24 & =2x+6 \\\\ 12x-24-2x & =2x+6-2x \\\\ 10x-24 & =6 \\\\ 10x-24+24 & =6+24 \\\\ 10x & =30 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ we accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1065 8781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32ce7197-6c97-5ece-b45d-186993605c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497892 #edb/ref 1080 "x=3" 